{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChK533vGqk1gXL8Mh6rLh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The issue's example now gives `['text-editor.js', 'text-editor-extras.js', 'color-picker.js']` with no warning, even with warnings turned into errors. But 15 existing tests in `forms_tests`, `admin_widgets` and `admin_inlines` now fail, because they check the old merge behaviour.\n\n**What was wrong:** `Media` merged its lists two at a time. Each in-between result added ordering rules that weren't really there. In the example, merging `color-picker.js` and `text-editor.js` first produced a list that \"required\" `color-picker.js` to come before `text-editor.js`.\n\n**The fix**, all in `django/forms/widgets.py`:\n- `Media.merge(*lists)` now takes any number of lists at once. It records only the real \"this file comes after that one\" pairs from each original list. It then sorts with `stable_topological_sort` from `django/utils/topological_sort.py`, keeping files in first-seen order wherever no rule applies.\n- `_js` and `_css` (per medium) now call it once over all the collected lists instead of folding pairwise.\n- The `MediaOrderConflictWarning` is only raised when the rules genuinely contradict each other. Its message now lists the input lists, e.g. `Detected duplicate Media files in an opposite order: [1, 2], [2, 1]`. In that case it falls back to first-seen order.\n\n**Failing tests** (I didn't change them, per the rules):\n- **Output order:** most of the `test_media.py` failures, plus `test_inline_media_only_inline`, expect the exact order the old algorithm produced. For example, the admin inline media now comes out as jquery, `my_awesome_inline_scripts.js`, `custom_number.js`, `jquery.init.js`, `inlines.min.js`. That still keeps every order the original lists required; it's just a different valid answer.\n- **Warning text:** `test_merge_warning` expects the old message format.\n- **List vs tuple:** the autocomplete `test_media` cases compare `_js` to a tuple, and it now always returns a list.\n\nThe order the sort picks matches Django's upstream fix as I remember it; I couldn't check that here. If the hidden tests expect the old ordering or messages, these differences will fail them."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":580,"cache_read_input_tokens":27001,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":580},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5a9f5d25-baf4-4cd6-a8d1-56ca622040e2","uuid":"8767aeca-64c2-4158-85f7-bfe42a967d4e","timestamp":"2026-11-23T02:28:05.492Z","request_id":"req_011ChK533naaixM28J8NAt5K"}
